  "result": "I wrote `record.jsonl` with a revised record under the same id, `drv.motion.right_turn`. I didn't withdraw or split it, because the record itself was sound.\n\n**What actually failed.** The gate rejected all three witnesses for the same reason: the coder's scooter trace was physically inconsistent. During the turn it gave the scooter a yaw rate of ∓18 °/s but left its recorded heading pointing east. The adapter can't produce a trace like that, since the yaw rate held over each one-second interval has to turn the heading by 18°. The body itself, `rises(band ∧ |yaw_rate| ≤ yaw_tol)`, was never judged.\n\nThe coder's note puts the failure down to a scope violation from a tool output file under `.runtime/tmp`, but this verdict is a witness failure. That `.runtime/tmp` problem may still need fixing in the harness's scope check.\n\n**Changes to the record:**\n- **Unchanged:** the contract text, formals, parameters, elements and act are word for word as before. I also added the wording the primitive's contract has about sparse sampling to the `prim.heading_change` justification.\n- **Scenario narratives:** each now says the recorded heading changes exactly as the yaw rate turns it.\n  - `holds`: −18 °/s for five 1-second steps, turning 18° clockwise per scene to face south, then yaw rate zero.\n  - `fails`: the same turn to the left, ending north.\n  - `blind`: the holds turn with `kin.yaw_rate` masked.\n  - The narratives also say the lead-in must be long enough that the window isn't truncated.\n- **`used_by`:** now only `LVMC_11_40_080_o1`, which is the only user the Users section lists. `LVMC_11_40_080_o2` was dropped.\n- **`differs_from`:** added against `drv.motion.u_turn`, because both take one track argument and read the yaw rate. U-turn covers a reversal to either hand; this concept covers only a right turn smaller than a reversal.\n\nThe coder also pointed out that no witness tests the `−u_turn_min` end of the band. I left that alone because the record is limited to three scenarios and none of them approach that end.",
